  "response": "The investigation of a catastrophic rupture begins at the fracture surface itself, where the first task is purely topographic. Fractographic examination traces chevron marks on the fracture surface back to the crack initiation site, and the broken sections are photographed at low magnification before any cleaning is attempted. Chevron marks on a fracture surface point away from the origin of the fracture. The diagnostic question is then posed as a strict alternative, of which exactly one branch can hold: either the crack initiation site lay within the weld metal or its heat-affected zone, or it lay in base metal outside the weld and its heat-affected zone. Once that fractographic mapping is complete, metallographic cross-sections are cut through the origin; these cross-sections reveal the weld bead and heat-affected-zone microstructure, which confirms that the crack initiation site lay within the weld metal or its heat-affected zone. Scanning electron microscopy distinguishes ductile dimpled rupture from brittle cleavage fracture.\n\nLocating the origin in weld material does not by itself date the damage, since a flaw at that position may have been present from the day the seam was laid down or may have grown there late in life. The next task is therefore to place the crack in time. Oxide scale within a crack thickens with increasing time of exposure to the service environment, which shows that crack-growth-rate analysis estimates the elapsed time between crack initiation and final rupture.\n\nIn the vessel at issue, at least one of two conditions obtained, and perhaps both: the seam weld contained a lack-of-fusion flaw dating from fabrication, or internal corrosion thinned the vessel wall during service. Attack of that corrosive kind can act only once the vessel has been put into operation, whereas lack-of-fusion flaws are introduced during the welding process at fabrication; it was that welding operation at fabrication which produced the lack-of-fusion flaw found in the seam weld, so the flaw was already in the vessel when it left the shop.\n\nThe documentary record is what converts this reconstruction into a finding. Original radiographic films from fabrication document the condition of the seam welds before commissioning, and the manufacturer's archive retained the complete set. Radiography reliably detects planar lack-of-fusion flaws lying parallel to the plate surface. Those films were exposed before the completed vessel was ever brought up to pressure; the hydrostatic proof-test records, compiled afterwards, establish the pressure the vessel sustained at commissioning. Taken together, the position of the origin, the microstructural character of the material around it, the time written into the crack and the fabrication and commissioning paperwork are what settle whether the vessel failed from a flaw it always carried or from metal lost while it worked.",
